# A Song for Life — Build a New Memorial Page

We are creating a new memorial page using the existing **A Song for Life memorial-page system** in the GitHub repository:

```text
zenacarina/a-song-for-life-pages
```

The existing working memorial pages, especially **Mary**, **Dennis**, **Margaret Rose** and **Peter Burgoyne**, are the model.

Peter Burgoyne demonstrates the optional **Read [Name]’s Story** section.

Do not redesign the page structure, shared template, styling, player, lyrics card, remembrance section, optional story section, QR code, footer or build system unless I specifically ask.

## Your task

Create a complete, ready-to-upload GitHub folder for the new memorial.

The folder should normally contain only:

```text
person-slug/
  data.json
  person-slug-hero.png
```

The same clean landscape image may be used for both:

```json
"heroImage"
```

and:

```json
"shareImage"
```

Do not create a separate social-share graphic with wording baked into it unless I explicitly request one.

## Important image rule

Once I have supplied or approved the final clean landscape image:

**Do not generate, redesign, edit or replace the image. Build the memorial page using it.**

The image should remain:

* text-free
* logo-free
* free from baked-in fades or cream panels
* suitable for both the page hero and social sharing
* landscape, with the person safely positioned away from the extreme edges

## Information I will provide

I will give you as much of the following as I have:

```text
Name:
Dates:
Final clean landscape image:
Audio URL:
Lyrics:
Life story or personal details:
Eulogy, questionnaire or longer notes:
Include a fuller Read [Name]’s Story section? Yes / No / Decide for me
Preferred short tribute line:
Preferred closing line:
```

Use the supplied material as the factual basis for the page.

Do not invent specific biographical details.

When a short tribute line, remembrance text, fuller story or closing line is missing, write suitable wording based only on the information provided.

## Writing requirements

The page should feel personal, dignified, warm and natural.

Avoid:

* clichés
* exaggerated spiritual claims
* generic funeral language
* wording such as “fly high”, “gained their wings” or “until we meet again”
* repeating the same idea excessively
* sounding like an advertisement
* turning the person into a flawless or unrealistic character
* inventing details that were not supplied
* reproducing another person’s writing word for word without clear permission

The short tribute line should normally be one or two concise sentences.

The **Remembering [Name]** section should normally contain three short paragraphs:

1. Their character, background or everyday presence
2. Their relationships, qualities and memorable details
3. How their influence, love or memory continues

Finish with one brief italic-style closing line.

## Optional fuller story section

When the family supplies substantial life-story material, questionnaire answers, notes or a eulogy, consider creating an optional:

```text
Read [Name]’s Story
```

section.

This section should be an original, edited life-story narrative based on the supplied information.

Do not reproduce a eulogy word for word unless I explicitly confirm that permission has been granted to publish it as written.

Use the fuller story only when it adds meaningful detail beyond the song and the shorter **Remembering [Name]** section.

The story should:

* preserve important names, dates, relationships and events
* remove repetition
* remove unnecessary administrative detail
* read naturally as a life story rather than a list of notes
* remain factual
* retain distinctive everyday details that make the person feel real
* be divided into readable paragraphs
* avoid repeating the shorter remembrance section unnecessarily
* remain collapsed until the visitor chooses to open it

The permanent page order is:

1. Song player
2. Lyrics
3. Remembering [Name]
4. Read [Name]’s Story — only when included
5. Share this memorial

The fuller story must appear **below the Remembering section**, not above it.

## `data.json` requirements

Create valid JSON following the structure already used by the working memorial pages.

Include the required fields:

```json
{
  "slug": "",
  "name": "",
  "shortName": "",
  "possessiveName": "",
  "dates": "",
  "pronouns": {
    "subject": "",
    "possessive": ""
  },
  "heroImage": "",
  "heroAlt": "",
  "heroShade": "",
  "tributeHtml": "",
  "songTitle": "",
  "audioUrl": "",
  "downloadUrl": "",
  "shareImage": "",
  "shareDescription": "",
  "lyrics": "",
  "rememberingTitle": "",
  "rememberingParagraphs": [],
  "closingLine": ""
}
```

When a fuller story is included, add:

```json
"storyTitle": "Read [Name]’s Story",
"storyParagraphs": [
  "First paragraph...",
  "Second paragraph...",
  "Third paragraph..."
]
```

The end of the JSON should then look like:

```json
"rememberingTitle": "Remembering [Name]",
"rememberingParagraphs": [
  "First remembrance paragraph.",
  "Second remembrance paragraph.",
  "Third remembrance paragraph."
],
"closingLine": "A brief closing line.",
"storyTitle": "Read [Name]’s Story",
"storyParagraphs": [
  "First story paragraph.",
  "Second story paragraph.",
  "Third story paragraph."
]
```

The story fields are optional.

When no fuller story is needed, omit both:

```json
"storyTitle"
```

and:

```json
"storyParagraphs"
```

completely.

Do not include them as empty values.

When `storyParagraphs` is included:

* `storyTitle` must also be included
* `storyParagraphs` must be a non-empty array
* every paragraph must contain meaningful text

Use:

```text
https://a-song-for-life-downloads.zenacarinas.workers.dev/person-slug
```

for the download URL, replacing `person-slug` with the actual slug.

Keep line breaks in the lyrics encoded correctly using:

```text
\n
```

Ensure:

* quotation marks are correctly escaped
* apostrophes do not break the JSON
* curly apostrophes are preserved correctly where appropriate
* the filename exactly matches the filename referenced in `heroImage`
* the filename exactly matches the filename referenced in `shareImage`
* the slug is lowercase and hyphenated
* the slug matches the GitHub folder name
* the finished JSON is valid
* no required field is accidentally omitted
* optional story fields are either both included or both omitted

## Working method

1. Review everything I provide.
2. Identify only genuinely essential missing information.
3. Ask one concise question only when something essential is missing.
4. Otherwise, proceed without unnecessary questions.
5. Write the short tribute line where needed.
6. Write the concise **Remembering [Name]** section.
7. Decide whether the supplied material warrants a fuller story section.
8. When it does, edit the material into an original **Read [Name]’s Story** narrative.
9. Keep the shorter Remembering section concise and avoid duplicating the fuller story.
10. Write the closing line where needed.
11. Create the valid `data.json`.
12. Use the final approved image without changing it.
13. Create the complete memorial folder.
14. Package the folder as a ZIP ready for GitHub.
15. Give me a working download link.
16. Tell me exactly where to upload it:

```text
a-song-for-life-pages/
  memorials/
    person-slug/
```

17. Tell me to unzip the download first.
18. Tell me to upload the entire `person-slug` folder into the GitHub `memorials` folder.
19. Suggest this commit message:

```text
Add [Name] memorial page
```

20. After it is committed, tell me to wait for the Cloudflare build.
21. Tell me to open:

```text
https://remembering.asongforlife.co.uk/person-slug/
```

22. Tell me to check the desktop and mobile versions.
23. Tell me to check that the song player, lyrics dropdown, optional story dropdown, download button, share button and QR code work correctly.

